{:edb/db-before-t 895 :edb/db-after-t 896 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534208 50 #inst "2026-10-11T17:46:13.449Z" 13194139534208 17592186045417 true] [17592186096459 1071 "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n![](images/49/49df59f984403cbbc994361c96313eca4d0875a7030dd997c90f1e393e23b69c.png)\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form, and then solve the linear system." 13194139534208 17592186045417 false] [17592186096459 1071 "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n![](images/b3/b3512e604d6c4246c5582a75360076115c4bd9b6701c42587f318254b1c026a3.svg)\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form, and then solve the linear system." 13194139534208 17592186045417 true] [17592186096459 1074 "Reducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now\n![](images/76/765eafb8a8b60aec9af512eeb666e2e13dbf83b8bc331b6effa75143930b4892.svg)\n\nand we can apply back substitution. The final equation $z=2$ tells us the value of $z.$ Substituting this value into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+(2)=-2\\,⟹\\,y=-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting both $y=-4$ and $z=2$ into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-(-4)+(2)=1\\,⟹\\,x=-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=-5,$ $y=-4,$ and $z=2.$ Or, in vector form, $\\langle -5,-4,2 \\rangle.$" 13194139534208 17592186045417 true] [17592186096459 1074 "Reducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now\n![](images/dd/dded05f0961034de6808d8f91f64afe8c367cec3c11fafdb26946090fa9c0891.png)\n\nand we can apply back substitution. The final equation $z=2$ tells us the value of $z.$ Substituting this value into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+(2)=-2\\,⟹\\,y=-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting both $y=-4$ and $z=2$ into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-(-4)+(2)=1\\,⟹\\,x=-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=-5,$ $y=-4,$ and $z=2.$ Or, in vector form, $\\langle -5,-4,2 \\rangle.$" 13194139534208 17592186045417 false] [17592186096460 1071 "Consider the system of linear equations\n![](images/0c/0c3483f97994da22dc0c739f9c3a26f87e070d28b684e63955a1129d00f9c755.png)\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form, and then solve the linear system." 13194139534208 17592186045417 false] [17592186096460 1071 "Consider the system of linear equations\n![](images/cd/cd75b39fc76773c0b3938ab6d60651e2b08dcd9ac325cc05718837934130ea07.svg)\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form, and then solve the linear system." 13194139534208 17592186045417 true] [17592186096460 1074 "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n![](images/93/933b897b7bc067610c87ea50a6fb871f04c5cc4610bd5bb0c868e6ffbac25e86.svg)\n\nReducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we get $a_{22}=0.$ Therefore, we swap the $2$nd and $3$rd rows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now\n![](images/42/42a1d61517661ccff31d9e603df548e79db7b0674a1f7f34c269a3a19e1c4bd6.svg)\n\nand we can apply back substitution. The third equation $z=1$ tells us the value of $z,$ and the second equation $y=3$ tells us the value of $y.$ Substituting these values into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+(3)+(1)=-2\\,⟹\\,x=-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=-6,$ $y=3,$ and $z=1.$ Or, in vector form, $\\langle -6,3,1 \\rangle.$" 13194139534208 17592186045417 true] [17592186096460 1074 "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n![](images/9f/9f1beb75e17aabdbeb5d814ae7ce3018f2ea89c36211070d40060d57445ca936.png)\n\nReducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we get $a_{22}=0.$ Therefore, we swap the $2$nd and $3$rd rows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now\n![](images/84/84de3f5eddf0a05d4f2d69aebd1537feb23a228fba3e1af31678dd4bdb1b59df.png)\n\nand we can apply back substitution. The third equation $z=1$ tells us the value of $z,$ and the second equation $y=3$ tells us the value of $y.$ Substituting these values into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+(3)+(1)=-2\\,⟹\\,x=-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=-6,$ $y=3,$ and $z=1.$ Or, in vector form, $\\langle -6,3,1 \\rangle.$" 13194139534208 17592186045417 false] [17592186096673 1038 #edb/ref 17592186414031 13194139534208 17592186045417 true] [17592186096856 1050 720.0 13194139534208 17592186045417 true] [17592186096937 1068 #edb/ref 17592186093596 13194139534208 17592186045417 false] [17592186096937 1068 #edb/ref 17592186414029 13194139534208 17592186045417 true] [17592186096937 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534208 17592186045417 true] [17592186096937 1071 "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by\n$M = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 & 19 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 13 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 2 & 25\\end{bmatrix}$.\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form, and then solve the linear system." 13194139534208 17592186045417 false] [17592186096937 1071 "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by \n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 & 19 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 13 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 2 & 25\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form, and then solve the linear system." 13194139534208 17592186045417 true] [17592186096937 1074 "Reducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 & 19 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 13 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 2 & 25\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 & 19 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 13 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 & 19 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 13 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4 & -20\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :=-\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 & 19 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 13 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x+y+2z=19 \\\\  & y+2z=13 \\\\  & z=5,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we can apply back substitution. The final equation $z=5$ tells us the value of $z.$ Substituting this value into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\ny+2(5)=13\\,⟹\\,y=13-10=3.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting both $y=3$ and $z=5$ into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx+(3)+2(5)=19\\,⟹\\,x=6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=6,y=3$, and $z=5.$ Or, in vector form, $⟨6,3,5⟩.$" 13194139534208 17592186045417 true] [17592186097841 1064 "Consider a system of linear equations and its respective augmented matrix $M\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y+z=6 \\\\ y-3z=-13 \\\\ -x-y-z=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can reduce $M$ to row echelon form using the standard Gaussian elimination algorithm. The algorithm that we use is very similar to the case of $2 \\times 2$ systems. \n\nOur goal is to create a descending \"staircase\" of $1$'s with $0$'s below, and we achieve this using elementary row operations. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & -13 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -1 & -4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & -13 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & -13 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -6 & -24\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :=-\\frac{1}{6}{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & -13 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix is now in row echelon form, and we can apply back substitution to solve the original system of equations.\n\n**Note:** The row echelon form of a matrix is *not* unique.  In the previous example, after the second row operation we arrived at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & -13 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -6 & -24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis augmented matrix is already in row echelon form! However, it is always useful to do an extra step and obtain $1$'s along the main diagonal, like we did previously:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & -13 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHaving $1$'s along the main diagonal makes the final back substitution process a little easier." 13194139534208 17592186045417 false] [17592186097841 1064 "Consider a system of linear equations and its respective augmented matrix $M\\mathbin{:}$\n![](images/67/673ea82819858b9a46e0e351a8d6f41b069ed039ee0d31a382110f938f7ef72a.svg)\n\nWe can reduce $M$ to row echelon form using the standard Gaussian elimination algorithm. The algorithm that we use is very similar to the case of $2 \\times 2$ systems. \n\nOur goal is to create a descending \"staircase\" of $1$'s with $0$'s below, and we achieve this using elementary row operations. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & -13 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -1 & -4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & -13 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & -13 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -6 & -24\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :=-\\frac{1}{6}{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & -13 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix is now in row echelon form, and we can apply back substitution to solve the original system of equations.\n\n**Note:** The row echelon form of a matrix is *not* unique.  In the previous example, after the second row operation we arrived at\n\n![](images/35/35627a034b30736abb057c01f3251fc047dce780a8cbcf389f8b5c2c2317d766.svg)\n\nThis augmented matrix is already in row echelon form! However, it is always useful to do an extra step and obtain $1$'s along the main diagonal, like we did previously:\n\n![](images/32/32a252e05e299a206f2f3776a2a04419f21726e1f8f542cfd4f328dac3251f21.svg)\n\nHaving $1$'s along the main diagonal makes the final back substitution process a little easier." 13194139534208 17592186045417 true] [17592186414019 1080 "M∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]" 13194139534208 17592186045417 true] [17592186414019 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534208 17592186045417 true] [17592186414019 1083 #uuid "1111935c-e645-5bcc-9a24-5310e4abf00c" 13194139534208 17592186045417 true] [17592186414020 1080 "⟨6,3,2⟩" 13194139534208 17592186045417 true] [17592186414020 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534208 17592186045417 true] [17592186414020 1083 #uuid "2af7fa69-5175-5f82-abdc-7a83d1c7437a" 13194139534208 17592186045417 true] [17592186414021 1080 "⟨-3,1,2⟩" 13194139534208 17592186045417 true] [17592186414021 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534208 17592186045417 true] [17592186414021 1083 #uuid "3faa2fb1-77d5-521f-acea-30296fef736c" 13194139534208 17592186045417 true] [17592186414022 1080 "M∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]" 13194139534208 17592186045417 true] [17592186414022 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534208 17592186045417 true] [17592186414022 1083 #uuid "48cbaac4-0396-5dd0-87a0-2f04eaf0760f" 13194139534208 17592186045417 true] [17592186414023 1080 "M∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]" 13194139534208 17592186045417 true] [17592186414023 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534208 17592186045417 true] [17592186414023 1083 #uuid "70ebcddf-aad2-5d94-849a-ae1ae9ab9ed7" 13194139534208 17592186045417 true] [17592186414024 1080 "⟨1,2,5⟩" 13194139534208 17592186045417 true] [17592186414024 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534208 17592186045417 true] [17592186414024 1083 #uuid "797daa95-425d-5598-93a6-9f8e22458433" 1319413953